    "narrative": "LawDog\u2019s lineup today hinges on exploiting the matchup against Zac Gallen, who serves as the catalyst for three of our strongest starts. **Jung Hoo Lee** leads the charge with a 68.02 score, buoyed by a 10% premium for his leadoff spot and a 10% platoon advantage. **Luis Arraez** (66.83) also benefits significantly from the SP skill matchup, as his contact-heavy profile excels against a pitcher with Gallen\u2019s profile. Meanwhile, **Lawrence Butler** (72.49) stands out as our highest-rated play; despite the uncertainty of a #7 lineup spot, his massive 18% SP skill boost and 13% platoon advantage against Walbert Ure\u00f1a make him an elite efficiency target. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains our core anchor (72.32), where his ability to capitalize on a slow delivery\u2014perfect for our SB-scarcity strategy\u2014combined with the favorable batting order, solidifies his status as a must-start despite facing a lefty in F. Valdez.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Gavin Sheets**, who posted a 44.76 efficiency score. While he held decent projection metrics, the looming threat of Yoshinobu Yamamoto (3.33 xERA) created a structural wall that pushed him out of the starting nine in favor of higher-floor assets like **Matt Chapman** (60.66), who despite a potentially lower spot in the order, remains a safer play against Gallen. We are monitoring **Will Smith**, **Tyler Freeman**, **Geraldo Perdomo**, **Drew Gilbert**, and **Matt Chapman** closely, as their scores are based on \"Lineup Pending\" assumptions. Should their confirmed batting positions shift downward, we may see a dip in their effective value. We continue to keep **Cal Raleigh** on the bench in his IL status, while maintaining depth with the likes of **Daulton Varsho**, whose score of 46.87 leaves him on the fringe; his utility is strictly conditional on his platoon leverage, and he remains a candidate for mid-game adjustment if the weather or in-game adjustments dictate a 